"modelAnswer": "Primary rocks, also called igneous rocks, are formed by the cooling and solidification of magma below the earth’s surface or lava on the surface. They are called primary because they form the original crustal material from which other rocks may later develop.\n\nCharacteristics:\n1. Molten origin: They originate from magma or lava, e.g., granite and basalt.\n2. Crystalline nature: Slow cooling produces large crystals as in granite, while rapid cooling produces fine or glassy texture as in basalt and obsidian.\n3. Hard and compact: They are generally massive, resistant and form landforms such as the Deccan Traps.\n4. Non-stratified and fossil-free: Since they form from molten material, they usually lack layers and fossils.\n5. Mineral-rich: They are associated with metallic minerals such as iron, copper, nickel and gold.\n\nTypes:\nA. Based on place of cooling:\n1. Plutonic/intrusive: Formed deep inside the earth; coarse-grained; granite, gabbro.\n2. Hypabyssal: Formed at shallow depths; dolerite, pegmatite.\n3. Volcanic/extrusive: Formed at surface; fine or glassy; basalt, obsidian, pumice.\n\nB. Based on composition:\n1. Acidic: High silica; granite, rhyolite.\n2. Intermediate: Andesite, diorite.\n3. Basic: Rich in iron and magnesium; basalt, gabbro.\n4. Ultrabasic: Very low silica; peridotite.\n\nThus, primary rocks form the foundation of the rock cycle and have immense geomorphological and economic significance.",
